1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,555
On October ninth, I asked ChatGPT the same
tax question twice.

2
00:00:04,555 --> 00:00:06,211
Same tool, same day,

3
00:00:06,211 --> 00:00:07,453
same tax year.

4
00:00:07,453 --> 00:00:12,835
One run said the standard deduction for
Indian students was fifteen thousand seven

5
00:00:12,835 --> 00:00:16,148
hundred fifty dollars. The other said
fifteen thousand.

6
00:00:16,148 --> 00:00:17,390
Both sounded confident.

7
00:00:17,690 --> 00:00:22,859
So I checked the official twenty twenty
five instructions for Form ten forty N

8
00:00:22,859 --> 00:00:28,028
R. Page seven lists fifteen thousand seven
hundred fifty as the filing threshold for

9
00:00:28,028 --> 00:00:30,613
single students eligible under the India
treaty.

10
00:00:30,613 --> 00:00:32,828
One run's number matched that figure.

11
00:00:32,828 --> 00:00:34,305
The other did not.

12
00:00:34,305 --> 00:00:36,890
Neither answer told me which to trust.

13
00:00:37,190 --> 00:00:39,059
I am an international student.

14
00:00:39,059 --> 00:00:39,807
Every year,

15
00:00:39,807 --> 00:00:45,040
students like me file Form eighty eight
forty three and work out whether a

16
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:50,273
tax treaty applies. The rules live in long
IRS publications that change every year,

17
00:00:50,273 --> 00:00:52,890
and a wrong answer has real consequences.

18
00:00:53,190 --> 00:00:55,780
So here is what I will build.

19
00:00:55,780 --> 00:01:00,590
Cite or Refuse: a tax helper that answers
only from official IRS publications,

20
00:01:00,590 --> 00:01:03,180
shows the exact passage and tax year,

21
00:01:03,180 --> 00:01:06,510
and refuses when they do not cover the
question.

22
00:01:06,510 --> 00:01:08,730
It will be a GitHub repository.

23
00:01:08,730 --> 00:01:10,580
None of it exists yet.

24
00:01:10,880 --> 00:01:12,517
I tested ChatGPT, Gemini,

25
00:01:12,517 --> 00:01:15,382
and Claude with the same three questions.

26
00:01:15,382 --> 00:01:17,838
A small test, one run each.

27
00:01:17,838 --> 00:01:23,569
None tied each claim to an exact IRS
passage for a stated tax year.

28
00:01:23,569 --> 00:01:26,025
Gemini cited only third party sites.

29
00:01:26,025 --> 00:01:27,662
Asked about my refund,

30
00:01:27,662 --> 00:01:28,890
none cleanly declined.

31
00:01:29,190 --> 00:01:30,667
Here is the plan.

32
00:01:30,667 --> 00:01:33,989
A pipeline will ingest several years of
IRS publications.

33
00:01:33,989 --> 00:01:36,204
Treaty tables will go into Postgres,

34
00:01:36,204 --> 00:01:38,050
so numbers are looked up,

35
00:01:38,050 --> 00:01:40,266
not generated. Retrieval finds the
passage,

36
00:01:40,266 --> 00:01:44,696
and an agent, through an M C P server in
Claude Code,

37
00:01:44,696 --> 00:01:47,280
answers only with what it can cite.

38
00:01:47,580 --> 00:01:48,717
Tax years matter.

39
00:01:48,717 --> 00:01:53,265
The IRS published clarifications to these
same twenty twenty five instructions after

40
00:01:53,265 --> 00:01:56,675
release. So every answer will carry its
tax year,

41
00:01:56,675 --> 00:02:01,981
and a new year's documents will go live
only after a human approves the

42
00:02:01,981 --> 00:02:02,360
update.

43
00:02:02,660 --> 00:02:07,091
Who opens it? A hiring manager for an AI
or data engineering role.

44
00:02:07,091 --> 00:02:08,454
In the first minute,

45
00:02:08,454 --> 00:02:10,158
they will run one command,

46
00:02:10,158 --> 00:02:14,248
ask a question and see a cited answer with
its tax year,

47
00:02:14,248 --> 00:02:17,316
ask something out of scope and see a
refusal,

48
00:02:17,316 --> 00:02:19,020
then check the evaluation scoreboard.

49
00:02:19,320 --> 00:02:21,312
What would that show about me?

50
00:02:21,312 --> 00:02:25,296
That I can build an AI system whose output
can be checked:

51
00:02:25,296 --> 00:02:26,624
prompt contracts, source grounding,

52
00:02:26,624 --> 00:02:27,952
a bounded agent loop,

53
00:02:27,952 --> 00:02:29,280
a small trained classifier,

54
00:02:29,280 --> 00:02:30,608
and held out evaluation.

55
00:02:30,608 --> 00:02:32,600
The evidence will be the tests,

56
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:35,920
the scoreboard, and a log of where it was
wrong.

57
00:02:36,220 --> 00:02:37,842
What it will not do:

58
00:02:37,842 --> 00:02:39,139
give personal tax advice,

59
00:02:39,139 --> 00:02:41,085
or prepare or file a return.

60
00:02:41,085 --> 00:02:44,004
What comes first: one tax year of one
publication,

61
00:02:44,004 --> 00:02:46,274
and ten held out questions through
retrieval.

62
00:02:46,274 --> 00:02:50,490
You will know it ran when the tests pass
on a fresh clone.

63
00:02:50,790 --> 00:02:52,328
What could sink it?

64
00:02:52,328 --> 00:02:55,403
Extracting tables from IRS documents can
go wrong,

65
00:02:55,403 --> 00:02:59,247
and a bad extraction would make a verified
number wrong.

66
00:02:59,247 --> 00:03:01,938
So I will start with one publication,

67
00:03:01,938 --> 00:03:05,013
check extracted values against the source
by hand,

68
00:03:05,013 --> 00:03:07,320
and mark anything unchecked as unverified.

69
00:03:07,620 --> 00:03:09,868
Why this project? It is personal,

70
00:03:09,868 --> 00:03:11,740
and it is a mix.

71
00:03:11,740 --> 00:03:14,737
Python, data cleaning, and testing I
already have,

72
00:03:14,737 --> 00:03:18,858
and my Reallocation Engine pull request
for this course shows them.

73
00:03:18,858 --> 00:03:20,356
M C P servers,

74
00:03:20,356 --> 00:03:21,854
retrieval evaluation, rules engines,

75
00:03:21,854 --> 00:03:25,600
and training a classifier from scratch are
new to me.

76
00:03:25,900 --> 00:03:28,435
Today, what exists is this plan,

77
00:03:28,435 --> 00:03:32,661
my test notes, and one check against an
IRS document.

78
00:03:32,661 --> 00:03:34,352
Everything else is will.

79
00:03:34,352 --> 00:03:36,887
Cite or Refuse. Swathi Baba Eswarappa,

80
00:03:36,887 --> 00:03:39,000
INFO seven three seven five.
